      "turn": 1,
      "user": "Can hempcrete hold up my roof, and is it recognized by U.S. residential code?",
      "assistant": "Hemp-lime (hempcrete) is not a structural load-bearing material, so it cannot hold up a roof on its own. Instead, it is used as a non-structural bio-composite insulation layer cast around a structural frame—such as timber, steel, or post-and-beam—that carries the actual weight of the roof and floors. \n\nRegarding U.S. building codes, hemp-lime is not yet part of the standard prescriptive International Residential Code (IRC). However, builders routinely gain approval through alternative methods or by working with local building officials using engineering design guidelines, AC555 standards, or code-modification requests.\n\nWould you like to explore what kind of structural framing is typically paired with a hemp-lime wall system?",
